rook: Give the camera tuning it can read

The camera HAL comes from checkers and reads its defaults through
GetCameraDefaultPara from libcameracustom_n, which only searches the
OV9734 sensor list. The GC0312 tuning from libcameracustom_rook never
reached it, the AE pline conversion included. Export
GetCameraDefaultPara from the shim so the HAL gets the rook sensor's
defaults.

The rook ISP tuning is in an older layout than libcameracustom_n reads:
with it, the picture is grey and overexposed by day and red at high gain.
Take the ISP tuning from the OV9734 instead. The rook 3A data has the
same layout, but its AE tuning overexposes with this AE algorithm, so
keep the GC0312's gains, exposure units and AWB calibration and take the
AE tuning from the OV9734.

The OV9734 ISP tuning takes a black level of 64 off. That cuts into the
GC0312's darker red and blue and turns the dim corners green, while the
rook tuning's 0 leaves a magenta cast. Take off 32, which leaves corners
and centre neutral. At the highest gain the GC0312 glows red towards the
top and the sides even with the lens covered; the ISP uses OBC set 3
there and set 2 in room light, so take off 64 in set 3 only, which
leaves a covered lens black.

The GC0312 runs at much higher gain than the OV9734, around ISO 1500
indoors, and the OV9734 colour matrices amplify its colour noise. Keep
60% of their distance from the identity, which cuts the colour noise by
a quarter and leaves the colours close to what they were.

In dim light the rook pline stops the exposure at 60 to 67 ms and
raises the gain instead. Lengthen those exposures by whole multiples, so
they stay in step with the mains flicker, up to 120 ms, and take the
gain down by as much. Indoors the exposure goes from 60 to 120 ms at
half the gain: the colour noise drops by another quarter and edges stay
sharp instead of being smoothed away, at 8 frames a second in dim light.

Light scattered in the small lens lifts the darkest parts of a picture
in a lit room to about a fifth of the range, which leaves it milky. The
ISP gamma curves map outputs below 220 of 1023 to black, with a soft
knee, and stretch the rest to the full range.

Measured on a Spot: a daylight frame went from an average luma of 207
to 143 with neutral colour, a covered lens at ISO 2982 from a red
average of 99 to black, and indoors the colour noise halved and the
darkest 2% of a board game box went from 70 to 18.
